---
title: Fire Cider
servings: 1
author: Margaret Boyles
source: https://www.almanac.com/how-make-fire-cider
source.name: The Old Farmer's Almanac
tags: [tonic, herbal, vinegar, preserving]
---

> Makes about 1 quart. Needs 3 to 4 weeks to steep before straining.

== Prep Ahead: For the Jar ==

Grate @horseradish{1/3%cup} and @ginger root{1/3%cup}.

Peel and dice @fresh turmeric{1/4%cup}, or use 2 tablespoons dried, powdered turmeric instead.

Mince @garlic{6%cloves}. Peel and dice @onion{1/2%cup}.

Split @habanero chilis{1-2}, or use cayenne pepper instead.

Slice @lemon{1%large} with the rind on (optional).

Measure @fresh rosemary{2%tbsp} and @fresh thyme{2%tbsp} (or 1 teaspoon each dried), and @black peppercorns{1%tsp}.

Chop @fresh parsley{1/2%cup} (optional).

Optional whole spices: a @cinnamon stick{}, a few @allspice berries{}, and a few @whole cloves{}.

== Prep Ahead: For Finishing ==

Measure @raw honey{1/4%cup}; adjust to taste when you sweeten.

== Cooking Day ==

Put the @horseradish{}, @ginger root{}, @fresh turmeric{}, @garlic{}, @onion{}, @habanero chilis{}, @fresh rosemary{}, @fresh thyme{}, @black peppercorns{}, and any optional ingredients (@lemon{}, @fresh parsley{}, whole spices) in a clean 1-quart #jar{}.

Fill the jar with @raw apple cider vinegar{1%quart} (at least 5% acidity), so it covers the herbs by an inch or two.

If the lid has metal parts, screw it on over a piece of #parchment paper{} or a small plastic bag to prevent corrosion. Seal tightly and shake well.

Steep for ~{3-4%weeks}, shaking daily or as often as you can.

== After Steeping ==

Strain out the plant material through a #strainer{}.

Sweeten with the @raw honey{} to taste.

Refrigerate and use within a year.

> Take 1 to 2 tablespoons daily in cold-weather months, diluted in water, tea, or juice. It also works in salad dressings, marinades, soups, and cocktails.
